Ivan,

Very few of the 486 desktop motherboards were capable of using 8MB 30-pin SIMMs,
mostly because the 8 meggers came along very late, about the same time that the
world made the transition to 72-pin SIMMs.

I have 4x4MB 80ns 30-pin SIMMs here for sure, but you don't want them to run a
33MHz 486.   Instead, you want 70ns SIMMs.   I recall that from the days when I
did a lot of work with 486 30-pin motherboards.

The Adaptec (1540 or 1542) may not work properly because the motherboard cache
is a write-back, marginally faster than the more conservative write-through.  If
the motherboard has a jumper to switch to write-through cache, try it.

Perhaps the best 486 30-pin SIMM motherboard I ever had was a full-sized AT
Micronics with 16 SIMM sockets, allowing a whopping 64MB of memory.

Let me see what I have in the way of 4x4MB 70ns 30-pin SIMMs.  I think I still
have some here.   Shipping across the pond may cost a little bit, but not a
terrific amount... Ben Myers

