I haven't  followed closely in recent years the development of cpu's, but
I always liked the IBM Power architecture.  Not only does it have a good
instruction set, but is designed for a more general market than, say,  
Alpha.
For example, it has one clock tick penalty for unaligned access, which is
important for languages like PL/I.  It is a pity the VAX architecture was
not extended to 64 bits instead of the Alpha folly.
