Tom Linden wrote:

(snip, I wrote)

>> I like VAX, too, but it was designed for the past, not the future.
>> Among others, a 512 byte page is too small, and the instruction length
>> is too variable, with too many and too complex addressing modes.
>> It was just at the point that memory prices were dropping fast, but
>> was designed for small memory systems of the past (pre 1976.)

> C'mon  Glen,  you can do better than that.  Roll back the clock 15 years
> and imagine you are put in charge of the task of taking VAX to the next  
> level,

Is that the question I was supposed to be answering?

> I know I could do it, and had I done it, it would be clocking about
 > the  same as AMD.  Page size, no problem, change it;

OK, so allow changes to supervisor state, but non problem state.
(IBM terms, sorry.)  That seems fair to me.

 > The addressing modes are great,
> I have used them all for code generation.  Of course the undelying micro-
> architecure would likely be some form of risc, but like 390 you would never
> see that. 

OK, I agree with that one.  I do like the addressing modes, but
that mostly matters for assembly programmers.  It helps a little
for compiler writers, but only a little.

> The problem with instructions set like Alpha is that they were  too
> low level, causing code bloat (about 3:1 versus VAX) thus demanding 
> ever  bigger caches, demanding bigger bandwidth to feed the monster.

You are getting close to my favorite complaint about just about all RISC
architectures: they depend on the compiler to generate optimal code.
That is fine, but it means that you can't update the processor without
recompiling all code.  What I would like to see is RISC software
distributed in an intermediate code, specific for the architecture
but not yet optimized for the sub-architecture.  The intermediate code
would be designed to make this final optimization easy.  It would then
be done either at install time or run time, whichever worked best.

But DEC tried too hard to make VAX code small.  It is easier to
make a larger cache than the complex decoder needed for VAX,
but yes, it could have been done.  But consider that IBM doesn't
market S/390 or z/ as desktop competition for x86.  DEC was
selling MicroVAX as desktop workstations, but priced much more
than x86/Windows.  Too much more.

Also, Sun did the transition from 680x0 (Sun3) to SPARC (Sun4).
They never got to a 68040 machine, either.  I don't remember big
complaints about that, though it did cause people to distribute for
both for a while.

 > Could you even  conceive of IBM 15 year ago announcing the RS6000
 > and announcing end of life for the 370 architecture, and telling
 > customers this is the great wave of the future,  and OBTW,
> all you have to do is to recompile, er.. well you might have to
 > rewite some things, and, er.. you will have to change all your
 > declarations so that everything  is strictly aligned

You mean FS?

 > (of course, IBM did a better job on alignment since  they  put in
> a one tick barrel shifter, but assume they hadn't) 
 > Now what would you expect to see happen to the customer base?
 > Well this is precisely what Digital did.
> No  wonder they effectively went bankrupt and got swallowed by Compaq.
 > BTW, I do believe  that Digital's owners have had relatively
 > little influence on the VMS organization.

Maybe IBM was lucky.  S/370 is a much better architecture to
extend in the way that was needed.  Only three instruction lengths,
a small number of address modes, 4K page size.  (The original option
of 2K page was lost somewhere along the way.)

> Moving to Itanium was not an option that could be rejected, however.

I think Itanium would have done much better if they left out the x86
compatibility.  If they are selling it for dedicated servers, those
can reasonably run all new code.  The result is too big and expensive
to do either well.  I wouldn't mind having one, but I wouldn't expect
to run Windows on it, either.

-- glen



