In article <eg18fi015k4@enews4.newsguy.com>,  <healyzh@aracnet.com> wrote:
>Carl Lowenstein <cdl@deeptow.ucsd.edu> wrote:
>> Now I discover that it won't turn on when I connect it to an AC outlet and
>> push the rectangular button on the upper left corner of the front panel.
>> Nothing happens.  Is there some interlock switch that I have overlooked?
>
>Is the CPU daughterboard properly seated?  I'd recommend pulling it out and
>reseating the card.  Be aware that this takes a *LOT* of force.

Following up on this and a lot of other good advice I received last
month.  Finally had some free time yesterday to work on the system again.
Learned that the "board extractors" for the XP1000 are not nearly as nice
as Qbus board extractors, since they don't help you put the card in again.

This month's observation, leaving me confused, is that the power supply
turns on and produces +5V and -12V (measured at a spare disk-drive
connector) but only if the CPU daughterboard is _not_ plugged in.
Or if the daughterboard is only half-way plugged in, because it took
me a few minutes to realize that it needed an extra push for the last
centimeter or so.  Shut down, push harder and watch the connector seat
fully, switch on again, nothing happens.

So there is something (hopefully logical) that is disabling the power
supply.  I did measure the CMOS battery on the motherboard and it seems
