> For example, it has one clock tick penalty for unaligned access, which is
> important for languages like PL/I.  It is a pity the VAX architecture was
> not extended to 64 bits instead of the Alpha folly.

Alpha was the VAX extension to 64 bits. The internal name was risce-vax
which was shortened to EV. Thus the various generations of Alpha called
EV-4, EV-5, EV-6 and EV-7. EV-8 was in the works, and had some really cool
stuff, but was killed by Compaq to grease the skids of the HP merger. HP
certainly couldn't bring in house technology that would surpass the Itanium
they had been jointly developing with Intel for way too many years.

While todays Itanium might finally match Alpha performance, it's the Y2K
Alpha that it's up against, as there's been no new development for half a
dozen years. I'll always wonder where Alpha would have gone if development
hadn't been terminated. 

