Alpha is not really a VAX extension but a new RISC architecture which emulates
some of VAX architecture functions (ex atomicity of certain operations) using
PAL code. The VAX architecture was very complicated especially if you see
addressing modes (but at the time of very small memory it was probably a good
thing). The VAX IS was so complicated that some instructions were not available
on the Cmos implementations (decimal instructions IIRW) but emulated in
software.

> While todays Itanium might finally match Alpha performance, it's the Y2K
> Alpha that it's up against, as there's been no new development for half a
> dozen years. I'll always wonder where Alpha would have gone if development
> hadn't been terminated. 

